<title>JAPAN: Japan coffee traders shrug off Colombia strike.</title>
<headline>Japan coffee traders shrug off Colombia strike.</headline>
<dateline>TOKYO 1996-10-17</dateline>
<text>
<p>Japanese coffee traders said on Thursday that a Colombian truck drivers' strike since earlier this week has so far had little impact on coffee shipments.</p>
<p>&quot;We still have enough coffee stocks at the ports, and see no immediate shipment problem,&quot; a coffee trader said. &quot;If the strike ends by the end of October, we'll have no worry.&quot;</p>
<p>The Drivers Association of Colombia went on strike on Sunday to press for higher tariffs, lower taxes and tolls, and tighter security on highways.</p>
<p>Exporters say the strike has halted all shipments of coffee and other key commodities to Colombia's ports.  </p>
<p>Japan is the third largest consumer of coffee after the United States and Germany. It imported 213,462 tonnes of green coffee during the first eight months of this year, Finance Ministry data showed. Of that, 47,153 tonnes came from Colombia.</p>
<p>But another coffee trader said that Japan's domestic coffee stocks have falled recently, and bean supplies from Colombia and countries in Central America are tightening.</p>
